1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,863
For my presentation today, I’m going to talk about the Céide Fields in the

2
00:00:06,863 --> 00:00:12,080
northwest of Ireland, one of the largest Neolithic sites in the world.

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:16,885
I recently visited this site and observed the work that is currently being done

4
00:00:16,885 --> 00:00:19,620
by a team of archaeologists there.

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:26,350
The site was first discovered in the 1930s by a local teacher, Patrick Caulfield.

6
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:31,387
He noticed that when local people were digging in the bog, they were constantly

7
00:00:31,387 --> 00:00:35,220
hitting against what seemed to be rows of stones.

8
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:40,200
He realised that these must be walls and that they must be thousands of

9
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:45,200
years old for them to predate the bog which subsequently grew over them.

10
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,088
He wrote to the National Museum in Dublin to ask them to investigate,

11
00:00:50,088 --> 00:00:52,300
but no one took him seriously.

12
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,148
It wasn’t until 40 years later,

13
00:00:55,148 --> 00:00:58,257
when Patrick Caulfield’s son Seamus,

14
00:00:58,257 --> 00:01:02,870
who had become an archaeologist by then, began to explore further.

15
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:08,366
He inserted iron probes into the bog to map the formation of the stones,

16
00:01:08,366 --> 00:01:13,942
a traditional method which local people had always used for finding fuel buried in

17
00:01:13,942 --> 00:01:16,630
the bog for thousands of years.

18
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:22,527
Carbon dating later proved that the site was over 5,000 years old

19
00:01:22,527 --> 00:01:26,820
and was the largest Neolithic site in Ireland.

20
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:31,476
Thanks to the bog which covers the area, the remains of the settlement at

21
00:01:31,476 --> 00:01:33,062
Céide Fields,

22
00:01:33,062 --> 00:01:38,180
which is over 5,000 years old, are extremely well-preserved.

23
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:40,306
A bog is 90 percent water;

24
00:01:40,306 --> 00:01:45,622
its soil is so saturated that when the grasses and heathers that grow on

25
00:01:45,622 --> 00:01:51,940
its surface die, they don’t fully decay but accumulate in layers.

26
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:56,658
Objects remain so well preserved in these conditions because of the acidity of the peat

27
00:01:56,658 --> 00:01:59,610
and the deficiency of oxygen.

28
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:05,008
At least 175 days of rain a year are required for this to happen;

29
00:02:05,008 --> 00:02:11,450
this part of Ireland gets an average of 225 days.

30
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:16,573
The Neolithic farmers at Céide would have enjoyed several centuries of relative peace

31
00:02:16,573 --> 00:02:18,040
and stability.

32
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:23,558
Neolithic farmers generally lived in larger communities than their predecessors, with a number of

33
00:02:23,558 --> 00:02:27,050
houses built around a community building.

34
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:33,660
As they lived in permanent settlements, Neolithic farmers were able to build bigger houses.

35
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:36,509
These weren’t round as people often assume,

36
00:02:36,509 --> 00:02:42,870
but rectangular with a small hole in the roof that allowed smoke to escape.

37
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:48,008
This is one of many innovations and indicates that the Neolithic farmers were the

38
00:02:48,008 --> 00:02:51,400
first people to cook indoors.

39
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:56,460
Another new technology that Neolithic settlers brought to Ireland was pottery.

40
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:02,050
Fragments of Neolithic pots have been found in Céide and elsewhere in Ireland.

41
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:03,794
The pots were used for many things;

42
00:03:03,794 --> 00:03:09,288
as well as for storing food, pots were filled with a small amount of fat

43
00:03:09,288 --> 00:03:14,200
and when this was set alight, they served as lamps.

44
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:20,960
It’s thought that the Céide Fields were mainly used as paddocks for animals to

45
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:21,010
graze in.

46
00:03:21,010 --> 00:03:25,437
Evidence from the Céide Fields suggests that each plot of land was of a

47
00:03:25,437 --> 00:03:28,830
suitable size to sustain an extended family.

48
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:34,106
They may have used a system of rotational grazing in order to prevent over-grazing

49
00:03:34,106 --> 00:03:38,520
and to allow for plant recovery and regrowth.

50
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:42,601
This must have been a year-round activity as no structures have been found which

51
00:03:42,601 --> 00:03:46,180
would have been used to shelter animals in the winter.

52
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:53,428
However, archaeologists believe that this way of life at Céide ceased abruptly.

53
00:03:53,428 --> 00:03:55,100
Why was this?

54
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:59,750
Well, several factors may have contributed to the changing circumstances.

55
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:05,240
The soil would have become less productive and led to the abandonment of farming.

56
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:11,422
The crop rotationsystem was partly responsible for this as it would have been very intensive

57
00:04:11,422 --> 00:04:13,070
and was not sustainable.

58
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:15,650
But there were also climatic pressures too.

59
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:20,074
The farmers at Céide would have enjoyed a relatively dry period,

60
00:04:20,074 --> 00:04:25,234
but this began to change and the conditions became wetter as there was a

61
00:04:25,234 --> 00:04:26,340
lot more rain.

62
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:32,500
It was these conditions that encouraged the bog to form over the area which

63
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:33,380
survives today.

64
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:36,710
So now I'd like to show you some...
